  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Personal Reflection\n",
    "\n",
    "- Xiaoyue Yu：\n",
    "\n",
    "As the team member responsible for the Safety sub-metric, my goal was to objectively quantify neighborhood safety for retirees. The process involved significant data cleaning and methodological refinement.\n",
    "\n",
    "The biggest technical hurdle was a data mismatch between the Crime and Census datasets. Inconsistent naming conventions (e.g., \"Mt. Oliver\" vs. \"Mount Oliver\") initially led to inaccurate \"zero crime\" calculations for several key areas. I resolved this by creating a name standardization map before merging the datasets, ensuring no data was lost. What's more, I realized that raw crime counts were biased against populous neighborhoods. To fix this, I normalized the data by population and filtered the data to focus only on \"Serious Crimes\".\n",
    "\n",
    "This refined approach revealed that large, populous neighborhoods like Squirrel Hill North are actually among the safest when adjusted for population, challenging initial assumptions based solely on incident counts. As Safety carried the highest weight in our AHP model, ensuring the accuracy of this metric was critical to our group's final success.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Liangyu Zhao:\n",
    "\n",
    "My target is measuring daily exposure levels and peak risks across different regions. Using daily exposure values from monitoring stations, I combined three key indicators—median, P90, and percentage of healthy days—through a minimum-maximum normalization process.\n",
    "\n",
    "The greatest technical challenge lay in spatial correlation. To map scores to communities, I taught myself ArcGIS software. After establishing effective community layers, I assigned each community the score of its nearest monitoring station, enabling direct comparison of our air quality analysis with team members' community-level metrics.\n",
    "\n",
    "Based on the BNAS score mapped to the “nearest monitoring station” using daily AQI data, Lawrenceville performs best in air quality metrics: it exhibits lower daily exposure (median), reduced tail risk (P90), a higher proportion of healthy days, and smaller fluctuations over time with fewer peak days. For residents—particularly retirees—this translates to cleaner and more predictable daily air quality. A key insight emerged: the choice of quantification method alters conclusions. Switching from simple ranking scores to the minimum-maximum scoring method preserved numerical differences while yielding more stable, interpretable results.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "- Boyi Sun:\n",
    "\n",
    "My analysis of the city's infrastructure revealed a fascinating trend: some smaller, less populous neighborhoods are actually 'hidden gems' when it comes to per-capita resources. \n",
    "\n",
    "By calculating the ratio of facilities to residents, I found that neighborhoods like West End and Hays offer an incredible density of safety and community amenities, which might otherwise be overlooked if we only counted total facilities. While I personally enjoy the vibrant, bustling atmosphere of Oakland for my current student life, the data objectively points to West End as a superior choice for a retiree prioritizing accessibility and resource availability. It scored the highest in my infrastructure sub-metric, showcasing its potential as a supportive community. However, in our final combined analysis, West End did not take the top spot. This discrepancy highlights a crucial lesson in data science: a single metric rarely tells the whole story. West End's lower scores in air quality and safety (relative to the top contenders) pulled down its comprehensive ranking."
   ]
  },
